# PlatformIO build artefacts
.pio/
.pioenvs/

# Editors
.vscode/

# OS clutter
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Local backups. data/config.json in the repo is the factory default and is
# meant to be committed; a snapshot exported from a running device is NOT —
# it contains the WiFi credentials and the generated hotspot password.
.backup/
*.bak
*.orig

# Release artefacts — the ZIP goes to GitHub Releases, not into the repo.
# (docs/flash/ IS committed: GitHub Pages serves the browser flasher from it.)
release/

# Local-only material that is deliberately not published
enclosure/

# ESP-IDF component manager output, left behind by the pioarduino build that
# compiles the Arduino layer from source: 34 components, 3,926 files, 534 MB —
# Zigbee, speech recognition, deep learning, Modbus and RainMaker, most of it
# prebuilt .a archives for chips this project does not even target (esp32c5,
# esp32h2, esp32p4).
#
# NOT project source, and nothing in this repository references it: grep over
# platformio.ini, extra_script.py and every script finds no mention outside the
# directory itself. Note that src/idf_component.yml declares only `idf: ">=5.1"`
# and none of these components, so do not assume they would be re-fetched on
# demand — ignoring them is safe (nothing is deleted), DELETING them is not
# something to do without a rebuild to confirm.
#
# Three reasons this must not be committed:
#   * 534 MB in a public history cannot be trimmed later without a rewrite.
#   * The tree carries real RSA and EC private keys — Espressif's own public
#     test fixtures (secure_boot.key, ca_key.pem, client.key, priv_key.pem in
#     six qemu_test folders), harmless in themselves, but GitHub Push
#     Protection can refuse the push over them, mid-release.
#   * 54 third-party LICENSE files would land under this repository's name
#     while THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md lists none of them — correctly so, since
#     none of it is linked into any firmware here.
managed_components/

# arduino-lib-builder scaffolding, same story.
.dummy/

# The simulator captures the boot screen on every start (sim_main.cpp, right
# after the first lv_timer_handler flush) because it is only on display for
# about 1.5 s and is otherwise impossible to look at. The call is
# unconditional, not behind --shots, and writes to the working directory - so
# building from the project root drops a fresh 900 KB bitmap here every run.
# Generated, never committed in v1.0.0, and nothing reads it back.
boot_shot.bmp
